      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment. It's part 3 of 5 of an OIML certificate (R 117/2007-A-NL1-22.02, project number 2638810, pages 8-10 of 15).\n\nThe content is primarily software version/checksum tables for various components:\n- 700 Core Processor (page 8 area)\n- 800 Enhanced Core Processor (page 8-9)\n- 820 Remote Dual Core Processor (page 9)\n- Software versions with no checksum (1700/2700, 2500)\n- 1700 / 2700 / 2500 (page 9)\n- 3500 / 3700 (page 10)\n- Part: Flow transmitter, Producer: Emerson Process Management Flow B.V., Type: 5700*1(2,3 or 5)A***ZZ*, Documentation folder: TC8519-4\n- Table 8 General characteristics of the flow transmitter type 5700*1(2,3 or 5)A***ZZ*\n\nSince this is a fragment, I should only include content present in this fragment.\n\nThe certificate info: Number R 117/2007-A-NL1-22.02 revision 0, Project number 2638810, Member State: The Netherlands, pages 8-10 of 15.\n\nThe schema says:\n- certificate: number, scheme (A|B), project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id\n\nNumber \"R 117/2007-A-NL1-22.02\" — scheme is \"A\" (from the -A- in the number).\n\nFor this fragment, the main content is:\n1. Software version tables → these fit best as `matrix_tables[]` entries.\n2. The flow transmitter part → components[] entry.\n\nLet me structure this:\n\n**matrix_tables**: For each software version table:\n- 700 Core Processor: columns version, checksum (×3 repeated). Footnotes: `*)` Indicated as 14AD, corrected from v3.2; `**)` Linearization feature.\n- 800 Enhanced Core Processor: same structure. Note about hardware versions and footnote `*)` Density Based Correction coefficients removed...\n- 820 Remote Dual Core Processor\n- Software versions with no checksum (1700/2700, 2500)\n- 1700 / 2700 / 2500\n- 3500 / 3700\n\nThe tables have a repeating (Version, Checksum) triplet structure. I could flatten them into rows with version/checksum pairs, or present columns as version_1, checksum_1, version_2, checksum_2, version_3, checksum_3. \n\nGiven the schema wants `columns[]` and `rows[]` (object per row), I'll use columns: [\"version\", \"checksum\", \"version_2\", \"checksum_2\", \"version_3\", \"checksum_3\"] — but that's awkward. Alternatively, since these are essentially lists of (version, checksum) pairs, I could restructure rows as one entry per pair. Hmm, but the schema says \"preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\" — the point is to preserve the table. I think flattening into a list of {version, checksum} pairs would be most useful, but the schema specifies rows as objects per row with keys from columns.\n\nLet me preserve the table structure as-is with columns [\"version\", \"checksum\", \"version_2\", \"checksum_2\", \"version_3\", \"checksum_3\"] and rows keeping the values. Actually, I think it's cleaner to normalize: since each row has 3 (version, checksum) pairs, and the pairs are unordered, I could list them all. But then rows would have inconsistent lengths.\n\nHmm. Let me think about what's most faithful and useful. The instruction: \"matrix_tables[]: preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere: name, columns[], rows[] (object per row), description, footnotes[]\".\n\nI'll preserve as-is with columns: [\"version\", \"checksum\", \"version_2\", \"checksum_2\", \"version_3\", \"checksum_3\"]. Footnote markers should be cleaned from values: \"F666*)\" → \"F666\" with footnote_markers? But matrix_tables rows are just objects per row — the schema doesn't say values must be structured objects there. In the R137 example, rows values are plain numbers. So footnote markers in matrix table cells... The footnotes section of the matrix_table can hold them. I'll strip the markers from values and note them in the table's footnotes, perhaps annotate in description.\n\nActually, looking at footnote markers in the tables: `2.6` with checksum `D732*`, etc. I'll keep values clean and record footnotes.\n\n**components[]**: The fragment mentions:\n- 700 Core Processor\n- 800 Enhanced Core Processor\n- 820 Remote Dual Core Processor\n- 1700 / 2700 / 2500 (software)\n- 2500\n- 3500 / 3700 (software)\n- Flow transmitter type 5700*1(2,3 or 5)A***ZZ* — Producer: Emerson Process Management Flow B.V., Documentation folder TC8519-4.\n\nHmm, the 700, 800, 820 are core processors (hardware components with software versions). The 1700/2700/2500 and 3500/3700 appear to be software parts. Actually wait — 1700, 2700, 2500, 3500, 3700 might be indicator models (e.g., Emerson/Micro Motion 1700, 2700, 2500, 3500, 3700 transmitters). Actually, Micro Motion 1700/2700 are transmitters, 3500/3700... Hmm. But here \"Software versions with no checksum: Part 1700 2700, Version 3.2, 3.3...\" and then \"1700 / 2700 / 2500\" version table with versions like \"5.0/1.0\" (application/bootloader pairs). And \"3500 / 3700\" similarly. These are likely software parts of those transmitter models.\n\nSince it's a fragment and I don't know the full component list from other pages, I should record what's here. I'll add components entries:\n\n1. role: \"core processor\", type_designations: [\"700\"], software versions table.\n2. role: \"enhanced core processor\", type_designations: [\"800\"], hardware versions v1–v6 with part numbers.\n3. role: \"remote dual core processor\", type_designations: [\"820\"], hardware versions v1–v2.\n4. role: \"software\", type_designations: [\"1700\", \"2700\", \"2500\"].\n5. role: \"software\", type_designations: [\"3500\", \"3700\"].\n6. role: \"flow transmitter\", type_designations: [\"5700*1(2,3 or 5)A***ZZ*\"], manufacturer: Emerson Process Management Flow B.V., documentation folder TC8519-4. Table 8 general characteristics — but table content not in fragment (cut off).\n\nActually, components schema: role, type_designations[], alternatives (\"OR\"|\"AND\"|null), characteristics{}. characteristics sub-values — the schema shows characteristics{} generally; for structured values rule 2 says every characteristic value must be an object with value/unit_symbol/unit_id/footnote_markers. Hmm, \"characteristics{}\" in components — I'll keep it minimal or empty since the details are in matrix_tables.\n\nActually, maybe I shouldn't overuse components. The main deliverable here is the matrix tables. But components are named sub-assemblies — \"include ONLY if cert names sub-assemblies\". The cert names: 700 Core Processor, 800 Enhanced Core Processor, 820 Remote Dual Core Processor, and the Flow transmitter 5700. These are sub-assemblies of the certified measuring system. I'll include them.\n\nFor the flow transmitter: type_designations: [\"5700*1(2,3 or 5)A***ZZ*\"]. Producer: Emerson Process Management Flow B.V. — but manufacturers[] is a document-level field. Should I add it to manufacturers[]? The fragment names the producer of the flow transmitter. Since this is a fragment of a larger cert and manufacturers may be defined on other pages, I'll include it in manufacturers[] since it's present in this fragment. Hmm, risky — but rule says extract what's present. The producer of the flow transmitter component is a manufacturer of a component, arguably. But document-level manufacturers[] would be the manufacturer of the certified type. Emerson Process Management Flow B.V. is likely one of them (the whole cert seems to be about Micro Motion/Emerson products — 700/800/820 cores are Micro Motion/Emerson). Actually 700 Core... these look like Emerson/Micro Motion transmitter cores. So Emerson is likely the applicant/manufacturer mentioned on page 1. I'll include manufacturers entry: name \"Emerson Process Management Flow B.V.\" — but wait, is that appropriate as \"manufacturer of the certified type\"? The certified type is probably a Coriolis meter system. The producer of the flow transmitter is Emerson Process Management Flow B.V. I'll include it in manufacturers[] as present in this fragment, since other parts may add more. Alternatively, keep it in the component entry. I think putting it in the components entry characteristics is safer, but the schema has no manufacturer field in components. I could put \"producer\" as a characteristic. Hmm.\n\nLet me be pragmatic: include manufacturers[]: [{name: \"Emerson Process Management Flow B.V.\"}]. Since this is a fragment, callers will merge. Actually the fragment is \"a fragment of one certificate\" — so the final answer will be combined. Including Emerson as a manufacturer seems reasonable given they're explicitly named as producer.\n\nHmm, but there could be a different overall applicant (e.g., Micro Motion Inc or Emerson Process Management). The instruction says don't fabricate; Emerson Process Management Flow B.V. is explicitly present. I'll include it.\n\n**certificate**: number \"R 117/2007-A-NL1-22.02\", revision is separate (revision 0 → revision_history? There's no revision history content here; the \"revision 0\" is just part of the number line). scheme: \"A\" (from \"-A-\" in number). project_number: \"2638810\". page_total: 15. member_state: \"The Netherlands\". oiml_issuer_id: NMi — the \"NMi\" appears as header. oiml_issuer_id field — NMi's OIML issuer ID... Not given explicitly as a number. Leave out or null. The NMi cert numbers often reference issuer \"NMi Certin B.V.\". I'll omit oiml_issuer_id since no explicit ID.\n\n**recommendation**: id \"R117\", edition 2007? The number \"R 117/2007\" suggests OIML R 117 edition 2007. Rule: id canonical e.g. \"R60\". Here R 117 → \"R117\". Edition: 2007. Accuracy classes — not in fragment. Scheme \"A\" — the recommendation scheme field could be \"A\". Hmm, scheme in certificate: scheme (A|B). R 117/2007-A → scheme A. I'll set certificate.scheme: \"A\" and recommendation: {id: \"R117\", edition: 2007}. Amendment: none.\n\nWait — should I be careful: \"R 117/2007-A-NL1-22.02\" — R117 recommendation, 2007 edition, scheme A, NL1 issuing branch, 22.02 certificate number. Yes.\n\n**footnotes[]**: Document-level footnotes:\n- `*)` \"Indicated as 14AD, corrected from v3.2\" (for 700 table)\n- `**)` \"Linearization feature\" (for 700 table)\n- `*)` \"Density Based Correction coefficients removed. To be used in gas application measuring Hydrogen or Helium.\" (for 800 table)\n\nNote there are duplicate `*)` markers with different meanings in different tables. In document-level footnotes, I'll list both (with markers as given). Maybe I'll keep footnotes within matrix_tables' footnotes instead, to preserve context. I'll do both: matrix_tables footnotes scoped, and maybe not duplicate at doc level. Actually schema has doc-level footnotes[]; but scoped footnotes in matrix_tables are allowed per schema. I'll scope them to the tables to avoid ambiguity of duplicate markers.\n\nAlso the \"Notes:\" under 800 and 820 tables — those are notes, which I can capture in matrix_tables.description or footnotes. The notes about hardware versions are important — I'll put them in the description of the respective matrix tables, or as characteristics of components. Let me put key notes into the components' characteristics/description.\n\nLet me now carefully transcribe the tables.\n\n**Table: 700 Core Processor** (Version, Checksum ×3):\nRow 1: 2.0 / 51FF; 2.7 / F666(*); 3.2 / 18D0\nRow 2: 2.1 / 2B3F; 2.8 / 1DEA(*); 3.3 / B0D1\nRow 3: 2.2 / 9005; 3.0 / D00D(*); 3.40 / 73A9\nRow 4: 2.3 / D75B; 3.0–ETO17153 / 97D6(*); 3.42 / F00C\nRow 5: 2.4 / 474F; 3.11–ETO19413(**) / 14AD; 3.50 / 11AA\nRow 6: 2.5 / 14AD; 3.12 / 1F1B(*); 3.52 / 3C4A\nRow 7: 2.6 / D732(*); 3.13–ETO18951 / 8BF8(*); (empty)\n\nFootnotes:\n- *): Indicated as 14AD, corrected from v3.2\n- **): Linearization feature\n\n**Table: 800 Enhanced Core Processor**:\nRow 1: 3.11 / 891378AB; 3.9 / 58CB3E0C; 4.51–ETO32353 / BC1660E8\nRow 2: 3.21 / 9893B999; 3.91–ETO21156 / 65F98DD7; 4.51–ETO33244 / D7B81135\nRow 3: 3.30 / A73D25DA; 3.94 / 47EB3E10; 4.60 / DDB76E3C\nRow 4: 3.42 / 7FA82CE8; 3.96 / 756C1BFD; 4.70 / AEB92E3F\nRow 5: 3.50 / D9343F05; 4.00 / C582F843; 4.80 / F1583A44\nRow 6: 3.52 / 132CCB63; 4.02 / 8D61C368; 4.9 / 6083BF9B\nRow 7: 3.6 / A9CA4E81; 4.14 / 40860C63; 5.08 / 4D368E71\nRow 8: 3.61–ETO17170 / 9AA358FF; 4.20 / 2983A9BE; 5.1 / 82C541D9\nRow 9: 3.7 / BE73CD62; 4.21–ETO21931(*) / D6349259; 5.2 / BD69FDD6\nRow 10: 3.71–ETO18982 / 580D32B6; 4.40 / B280233F; 5.22 / F4A8D922\nRow 11: 3.8 / 8CA8E7D1; 4.42 / D7BA0841; 5.23–ETO45214 / B1D70450\nRow 12: 3.81–ETO20775 / 7931CE3D; 4.50 / 6B48C624; 5.30 / 65828884\n\nNotes (description):\n- For 800ECP v3.80 or higher: writing ETO number 13511 in Modbus register 5005 enables linearization feature.\n- 800 ECP versions lower than 5.xx are not compatible with hardware version v4...\n- Hardware versions:\n  - 800 v1: part number 20006069\n  - 800 v2: part number 20016889 (same as v1 with RoHS components)\n  - 800 v3: part number MMI-20020807, rev AD\n  - 800 v4: part number MMI-20020807, rev. AE (uses SSM2604 CODEC from different supplier; updated board electronics; software 5.00+ backwards compatible)\n  - 800 v5: part number MMI-20088230, rev. AA (same CODEC as v4 with added compensation circuit...)\n  - 800 v6: part number MMI-20093828, rev. AA (uses ADAU1361 CODEC instead of SSM2604; needs software 5.00+)\n- See documentation folder, section 800 Core Processor\n\nFootnote: *): Density Based Correction coefficients removed. To be used in gas application measuring Hydrogen or Helium.\n\n**Table: 820 Remote Dual Core Processor**:\nRow 1: 1.00 / 52FB 1CF0; 1.30 / AC56C460; 1.50 / F42A4B2C\nRow 2: 1.10 / 787951AA; 1.40 / 8B64EF94; —\nRow 3: 1.20 / 3B7249F6; 1.41 / 073C45F2; —\n\nNotes:\n- Fuel Consumption feature from v1.10 onwards is not approved.\n- 820 versions lower than 1.50 not compatible with hardware v2. Hardware versions:\n  - 820 v1: part number MMI-200229698 Rev. AB\n  - 820 v2: part number MMI-20029698 Rev. AC (uses SSM2604 CODEC from different supplier; updated board electronics; software 1.50+ backwards compatible)\n- See documentation folder, section 820 Dual Core-Processor\n\n**Table: Software versions with no checksum**:\nColumns: Part, Version, Remark\nRow: \"1700\\n2700\" → [\"1700\", \"2700\"]; versions: 3.2, 3.3, 3.4, 3.4.1, 3.5.3, 3.6, 3.7, 4.1, 4.2; Remark: \"\"\nRow: 2500; versions: 4.0, 4.1, 4.2; Remark: \"\"\n\n**Table: 1700 / 2700 / 2500**:\nRow 1: 5.0/1.0 / 7A7F0B39; 6.4/1.3 / B77B25C9; 7.1/1.3 / 88FB1B5C\nRow 2: 5.1/1.0 / 95F0BC47; 6.5/1.3 / 88FB1B5C; 7.2/1.3 / 9ECE81F1\nRow 3: 5.12/1.0 / A14FBFB9; 6.6/1.3 / 9ECE81F1; 7.3/1.3 / 4A5365D4\nRow 4: 5.2/1.0 / 746CBE79; 6.7/1.3 / 4A5365D4; 8.0/1.3 / 1E1467F9\nRow 5: 6.0/1.1 / BB615B55; 6.8/1.3 / 1E1467F9; 8.02/1.3 / 201465F9\nRow 6: 6.1/1.2 / 13176BE6; 6.82/1.3 / 201465F9; —\nRow 7: 6.11–ETO19266 / 9B13F21A; 7.0/1.3 / B77B25C9; —\n\n**Table: 3500 / 3700**:\nRow 1: 7.0/1.1 / A1C34F1C; 8.1/1.3 / 4279A001; 8.41–ETO26097 / 31D36D05\nRow 2: 7.1/1.1 / D5783FCF; 8.14/1.3 / 62F125F2; 8.43–ETO31478 / E35DF3C0\nRow 3: 7.2/1.1 / 20609FD3; 8.2/1.4 / 368139C5; 8.50/1.5 / 1C146AF7\nRow 4: 8.0/1.2 / 158A12BD; 8.21–ETO23686 / D507F464; 8.51-ETO22243 / B18A0CB3\nRow 5: 8.02–ETO18947 / 1CC007C4; 8.3/1.4 / 8F65A9E9; —\nRow 6: 8.03–ETO19299 / 2D6104C2; 8.4/1.4 / 227B10D2; —\n\nThen: \"See documentation folder page 7057/35-01 with an overview of the ETO Software for the different transmitters.\"\n\nThen: Part: Flow transmitter; Producer: Emerson Process Management Flow B.V.; Type: 5700*1(2,3 or 5)A***ZZ*; Documentation folder: TC8519-4; Table 8 General characteristics of the flow transmitter... (cut off).\n\nNow for the matrix_tables representation: Should I flatten the three-pair rows into individual version/checksum pairs? The table is essentially a list. I think preserving the row structure is most faithful (\"preserve multi-dim tables\"). I'll use columns [\"version\", \"checksum\", \"version_2\", \"checksum_2\", \"version_3\", \"checksum_3\"] and rows as objects.\n\nHmm, alternatively flatten each (version, checksum) pair into its own row: columns [\"version\", \"checksum\"], rows = flat list. That loses the visual layout but is semantically equivalent and cleaner. The instruction for R137 preserved 2D layout. For version/checksum lists, the layout is just wrapping. Semantically, flattening is fine and more useful. But \"preserve\" suggests fidelity. I'll go with preserving the 3-pair row layout to be safe? Hmm...\n\nActually, let me think about what the consumer wants. The schema is for extracting data about instruments. Software version/checksum tables are auxiliary metadata. matrix_tables exists to preserve them. Either representation preserves data. I'll preserve row layout as in the source (6 columns), because \"preserve multi-dim tables\" is the stated purpose. But rows would be objects with keys version, checksum, version_2, checksum_2, version_3, checksum_3. That's a bit ugly but faithful.\n\nAlternatively I could do columns [\"version\", \"checksum\"] and rows as pairs, with a description noting the original layout had 3 pairs per row. That's cleaner and lossless in content. I'll go with flattened pairs — no wait. Let me just preserve as-is. Hmm.\n\nDecision: flatten to pairs. Reasons: (1) each pair is independent; (2) easier to merge/validate; (3) description can note original 3-column layout. Actually, I keep flip-flopping. Let me consider the footnote markers: some checksums have markers — in flattened form each row can have footnote info? Rows are objects; I can't attach footnote_markers per cell in a plain object... I could include the marker in the value like \"F666\" and separately list footnotes. The schema for matrix_tables rows doesn't support structured values (the R137 example uses plain values). So markers must be stripped and footnotes listed. But then which cells had markers is lost. To preserve that, I could keep the marker in the value string? No — rule 1 says never include footnote markers in labels; rule 2 is about characteristics. For matrix tables, keeping \"F666\" clean is right; the footnote text explains. But the association (which versions had * ) is lost. Hmm — actually the footnotes reference specific content (\"Indicated as 14AD, corrected from v3.2\" — mentions v3.2; \"Linearization feature\" — 3.11–ETO19413). The footnote text itself references versions, so the information is recoverable-ish.\n\nI'll preserve the 6-column layout to keep row association minimal loss, and strip markers, and add footnotes with reference in description... no. OK final decision: preserve 6-column layout. It's the most faithful \"preserve\" approach and the schema example shows rows as objects with keys from columns, supporting multi-column rows.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually let me reconsider once more. The R137 example: columns [\"size\", \"qmax\", ...], rows objects. A version/checksum table with 6 columns where headers repeat (\"Version\",\"Checksum\",\"Version\",\"Checksum\",\"Version\",\"Checksum\") — keys must be unique in JSON objects. So I'd use version_1/checksum_1/version_2/checksum_2/version_3/checksum_3 or version/checksum/version_2/checksum_2/version_3/checksum_3. Fine.\n\nRows with empty trailing cells: include as null.\n\nNow the \"Software versions with no checksum\" table: columns [\"part\", \"version\", \"remark\"], rows: {part: [\"1700\",\"2700\"], version: [\"3.2\",\"3.3\",\"3.4\",\"3.4.1\",\"3.5.3\",\"3.6\",\"3.7\",\"4.1\",\"4.2\"], remark: null} and {part: [\"2500\"], version: [\"4.0\",\"4.1\",\"4.2\"], remark: null}. Values as arrays — fine since rows are objects.\n\nNow components. Let me define:\n\n```json\n\"components\": [\n  {\n    \"role\": \"core processor\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"700 Core Processor\"]\n  },\n  {\n    \"role\": \"enhanced core processor\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"800 Enhanced Core Processor\"],\n    \"characteristics\": {\n      \"hardware_versions\": ...\n    }\n  },\n  ...\n]\n```\n\nHmm, characteristics{} within components — rule 2 says every characteristic value must be a structured object. That applies to \"characteristic value\". For component characteristics like hardware version part numbers... This gets heavy. Maybe simpler: put hardware version info in the matrix_tables description, and keep components light with just role + type_designations.\n\nAlternatively, model_family? No — these aren't model variants of the certified type; they're component firmware versions. matrix_tables is the right home.\n\nI'll include components with role and type_designations, and minimal characteristics where clean (e.g., flow transmitter producer/documentation folder). Actually for the flow transmitter:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"role\": \"flow transmitter\",\n  \"type_designations\": [\"5700*1(2,3 or 5)A***ZZ*\"],\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"producer\": {\"value\": \"Emerson Process Management Flow B.V.\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n    \"documentation_folder\": {\"value\": \"TC8519-4\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null}\n  }\n}\n```\n\nHmm, \"producer\" as characteristic is odd. But schema allows characteristics{} in components. Alternatively add manufacturers[] entry. I'll do both? Adding Emerson Process Management Flow B.V. to manufacturers[] — the rule about party layouts says parse Manufacturer labels. Here the label is \"Producer\". It is a manufacturer of a certified component. I'll include it in manufacturers[] since it's clearly a manufacturer named in this fragment. And also keep it out of component characteristics to avoid duplication... Actually, I'll keep it in manufacturers[] only.\n\nWait, but is Emerson Process Management Flow B.V. the manufacturer of the certified type overall? This cert is R117 (automatic level gauges / or \"Measuring systems for gaseous...\". Actually R117 is \"Non-automatic weighing instruments\"? No — R117 is \"Measuring systems for liquef...\"? Let me recall: OIML R117 is \"Dynamic measuring systems for liquids other than water\" (fuel dispensers etc.). Yes! R 117 = \"Dynamic measuring systems for liquids other than water\". With flow transmitter 5700 (Micro Motion Coriolis), cores 700/800/820, indicators 1700/2700/2500/3500/3700 — this is a Micro Motion/Emerson Coriolis meter system certified under R117 scheme A. So Emerson Process Management (Flow B.V. is the Netherlands flow division) is plausibly the applicant/manufacturer. Including it in manufacturers[] is reasonable.\n\nNow certificate block:\n\n```json\n\"certificate\": {\n  \"number\": \"R 117/2007-A-NL1-22.02\",\n  \"scheme\": \"A\",\n  \"project_number\": \"2638810\",\n  \"page_total\": 15,\n  \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\"\n}\n```\n\nrevision: \"revision 0\" — the schema has revision_history[] but that's for revisions of the cert with dates/changes. \"revision 0\" in the header is the current revision. I could omit revision_history. Should I record revision 0 somewhere? Schema doesn't have a \"revision\" field at certificate level. revision_history entries have revision, date, changes. There's no date/changes given. I'll omit revision_history.\n\nissuing_authority: name \"NMi\" — present as header. Address etc. not in fragment. I'll include issuing_authority: {name: \"NMi\"}. Hmm, should I? The fragment shows \"NMi\" headers. It's fine to include name only. Or omit. I'll include name.\n\nrecommendation: {id: \"R117\", edition: 2007}. Should scheme be in recommendation? Schema: recommendation: id, edition, amendment, scheme, accuracy_classes. scheme: \"A\". accuracy_classes: not present. \n\ncertified_type: category — likely \"Measuring system\" but not stated in fragment. Don't fabricate → omit certified_type? The flow transmitter is a component. The 700/800/820 cores are components. Category of whole cert isn't stated here. Omit certified_type. Hmm, but components are \"named sub-assemblies\" — I can include components[] without certified_type.\n\nNow footnotes at document level: The three footnote definitions. Since duplicate markers exist, maybe scope them within matrix_tables footnotes. I'll put them in the respective matrix_tables entries' footnotes arrays with markers \"*\" and \"**\". Marker text: \"*\". Keep original \"*\"? The markdown shows `\\*)`. I'll use \"*\" and \"**\".\n\nLet me now also capture the notes in matrix_tables descriptions:\n\n700 table description: null (no notes besides footnotes).\n800 table description: \"For the 800ECP v3.80 or higher: by writing ETO number 13511 in Modbus register 5005, the linearization feature is enabled. 800 ECP versions lower than 5.xx are not compatible with hardware version v4 of the Enhanced Core Processor. Hardware versions: 800 v1 part number 20006069; 800 v2 part number 20016889 (same as v1 but with RoHS components); 800 v3 part number MMI-20020807 rev AD; 800 v4 part number MMI-20020807 rev AE (uses SSM2604 CODEC from a different supplier, updated board electronics, software version 5.00 and higher required, backwards compatible with v1–v3); 800 v5 part number MMI-20088230 rev AA (same CODEC as v4 with added compensation circuit and removal of unused board parts); 800 v6 part number MMI-20093828 rev AA (uses ADAU1361 CODEC instead of SSM2604, needs software 5.00 and higher). See the documentation folder, section 800 Core Processor.\"\n\nHmm — that's long. Alternatively, put hardware versions into component characteristics. Let me think: the 800 hardware versions are genuinely component configuration info. components[] entry with characteristics:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"role\": \"enhanced core processor\",\n  \"type_designations\": [\"800\"],\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"hardware_version_v1_part_number\": {\"value\": \"20006069\", ...},\n    ...\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThat's awkward. Simpler: keep components minimal and store notes in matrix_tables.description as a string. I'll do that. Actually — maybe I can add a separate matrix_table for hardware versions? That'd be fabricating structure not in source. No.\n\nOK let me also consider: should the components include the software parts 1700/2700/2500 and 3500/3700? These seem to be \"Parts\" in the software table — likely indicator/transmitter models (Micro Motion 1700, 2700, 2500, 3500, 3700 are field/transmitter... actually Micro Motion 1700/2700 are transmitters, 2500, 3500/3700...). Without more context, I'll treat them as software parts with matrix tables referencing them. I could add components entries with role \"software (application)\" etc. Hmm. The table headers \"1700 / 2700 / 2500\" and \"3500 / 3700\" are part names. I'll add components entries:\n\n- role: \"core processor\", type_designations: [\"700\"]\n- role: \"enhanced core processor\", type_designations: [\"800\"]\n- role: \"remote dual core processor\", type_designations: [\"820\"]\n- role: \"software part\", type_designations: [\"1700\", \"2700\", \"2500\"]\n- role: \"software part\", type_designations: [\"3500\", \"3700\"]\n- role: \"flow transmitter\", type_designations: [\"5700*1(2,3 or 5)A***ZZ*\"]\n\nHmm, for the 5700 the asterisks are wildcards: \"5700*1(2,3 or 5)A***ZZ*\" — meaning 5700 followed by wildcard, 1, then (2, 3 or 5), A, wildcards, ZZ, wildcard. Keep the designation verbatim.\n\nAlternatively, should the components list be left out entirely and everything in matrix_tables? The cert names these sub-assemblies, so components[] is warranted. But is 5700 a \"sub-assembly\" or the certified instrument itself? R117 measuring systems for liquids — a Coriolis meter consists of sensor + transmitter. The flow transmitter 5700 is a component. Fine.\n\nActually wait — maybe the whole certified thing is a fuel/liquid measuring system with components: flow sensor, flow transmitter 5700, core processors (700/800/820), etc. This fragment is the software/firmware section plus start of flow transmitter section.\n\nNow, let me decide matrix_tables entries:\n\n1. name: \"700 Core Processor software versions\", columns: [\"version\", \"checksum\", \"version_2\", \"checksum_2\", \"version_3\", \"checksum_3\"], rows: 7 rows, footnotes: [{\"marker\": \"*\", \"text\": \"Indicated as 14AD, corrected from v3.2\"}, {\"marker\": \"**\", \"text\": \"Linearization feature\"}], description: null.\n\nWait — footnote \"*: Indicated as 14AD, corrected from v3.2\". Marked cells: checksums F666 (v2.7), 1DEA (v2.8), D00D (v3.0), 97D6 (v3.0–ETO17153), 1F1B (v3.12), 8BF8 (v3.13–ETO18951), D732 (v2.6). And ** on 3.11–ETO19413 version. Since rows are plain objects, I lose which cells had markers. Option: include marker in the value string? Violates cleanliness. Option: add footnote markers arrays per row? Rows are objects; I could add a \"footnote_markers\" key per row: e.g. {\"version\": \"2.7\", \"checksum\": \"F666\", \"footnote_markers\": [\"*\"]}? That's hacky but preserves association partially. Hmm.\n\nActually, maybe I should flatten to (version, checksum) pair rows and add a footnote_markers field per row. That handles association cleanly:\n\n```json\n\"rows\": [\n  {\"version\": \"2.0\", \"checksum\": \"51FF\"},\n  {\"version\": \"2.7\", \"checksum\": \"F666\", \"footnote_markers\": [\"*\"]},\n  ...\n]\n```\n\nBut then the row structure is flattened vs original 3-per-row layout. The schema says rows is \"object per row\" — flattening changes rows. I think content fidelity matters more than layout fidelity. But \"preserve\" suggests fidelity. Ugh.\n\nLet me go with preserving the 6-column layout and NOT per-cell markers; list footnotes in footnotes[]. The footnote text itself is preserved. I'll mention in description? No — keep it simple. Actually, you know, I could do both: keep 6-column layout, and rows are objects; where a cell has a marker, I... can't attach. Fine — footnotes listed, association implicit.\n\nHmm, alternatively preserve layout AND append a per-row \"footnote_markers\" key listing markers present in that row: e.g. row 1 of 700: version 2.7 checksum F666* → footnote \"*\". Row-level markers list like [\"*\"] without cell association. It's ambiguous (which cell). I'd rather not.\n\nFinal: 6-column layout, clean values, footnotes array. Done deliberating.\n\n2. name: \"800 Enhanced Core Processor software versions\", same columns, 12 rows, footnotes: [{\"marker\": \"*\", \"text\": \"Density Based Correction coefficients removed. To be used in gas application measuring Hydrogen or Helium.\"}], description: the notes.\n\n3. name: \"820 Remote Dual Core Processor software versions\", same columns, 3 rows (with nulls), description: notes.\n\n4. name: \"Software versions with no checksum\", columns [\"part\", \"version\", \"remark\"], rows 2, description: null.\n\n5. name: \"1700 / 2700 / 2500 software versions\", same 6 columns, 7 rows.\n\n6. name: \"3500 / 3700 software versions\", same 6 columns, 6 rows.\n\nAlso the note \"See documentation folder page 7057/35-01 with an overview of the ETO Software for the different transmitters.\" — attach to 3500/3700 table description or as general. I'll append to 3500/3700 description.\n\nNow, empty cells: last row of 700 has empty cells → null. Same for others.\n\n820 row values: \"52FB 1CF0\" — keep as string with space? Original \"52FB 1CF0\". I'll keep \"52FB 1CF0\".\n\nAlso row entries: for 800 table row 9: version \"4.21–ETO21931\" has marker *, checksum \"D6349259\" clean.\n\nEn-dashes in \"3.0–ETO17153\": keep as is.\n\n\"8.51-ETO22243\" uses hyphen; keep as-is.\n\nNow the model_family: not applicable here — these are component software versions, not model variants of the certified type. Skip.\n\ncharacteristics: type_level — nothing here. model_level — nothing. config_level — nothing. Skip characteristics entirely? The fragment has no quantitative characteristics. Skip.\n\nrevision_history: skip.\n\nfootnotes[] at doc level: I decided to scope footnotes to matrix_tables. But schema also has doc-level footnotes[]. Since duplicate \"*\" markers with different texts, doc-level would be ambiguous. I'll scope within tables only. That's allowed.\n\nissuing_authority: {name: \"NMi\"}. Hmm — NMi header appears. Include name only. Actually, is \"NMi\" the issuing authority? Yes (Netherlands